{"name":"Flowerhorn Cichlid Red","hex":"#f62e52","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flowerhorn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A brightly-coloured ornamental cichlid with a distinctive nuchal hump."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flowerhorn"},{"term":"cichlid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many tropical fish, of the family Cichlidae, popular as aquarium fish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cichlid"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flowerhorn cichlid","blocks":[{"text":"Flowerhorn cichlids are ornamental aquarium fish noted for their vivid colors and the distinctively shaped heads for which they are named. Their head protuberance is formally called a nuchal hump. Like blood parrot cichlids, they are hybrids that exist in the wild only because of their release. Flowerhorns first emerged for sale on the aquarium market in Malaysia in the late 1990s and soon became popular in many countries in Asia. They are commonly kept by hobbyists in the US, Asia, and Europe. Numerous cast-off flowerhorns have been released to the wild, especially in Singapore and Malaysia, where they have become an invasive pest animal. Their importation is banned in Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gallery_19_68_148247.jpg/330px-Gallery_19_68_148247.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flowerhorn_cichlid"},{"title":"Cichlid","blocks":[{"text":"Cichlids are a large, diverse, and widespread family of percomorph fish in the family Cichlidae, order Cichliformes. At least 1,760 species have been scientifically described, making it one of the largest vertebrate families, with only the Cyprinidae being more speciose. New species are discovered annually, and many species remain undescribed. The actual number of species is therefore unknown, with estimates varying between 2,000 and 3,000. They are native to the Neotropics, Africa, the Middle East, and the Indian subcontinent, although some species have been introduced worldwide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Sea_Life_Centre%2C_Blackpool_2016_009_-_Malawi_Cichlid.jpg/330px-Sea_Life_Centre%2C_Blackpool_2016_009_-_Malawi_Cichlid.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cichlid"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Post-77-1231053436.gif/500px-Post-77-1231053436.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Post-77-1231053436.gif/500px-Post-77-1231053436.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"General flowerhorn variety classification, containing several subsets of varieties (strains) from different countries and breeders","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APost-77-1231053436.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Post-19-1231048027.jpg/250px-Post-19-1231048027.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Post-19-1231048027.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Post-19-1231048027.jpg/250px-Post-19-1231048027.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Golden Monkey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APost-19-1231048027.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Kingkamfa.jpeg/330px-Kingkamfa.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Kingkamfa.jpeg/330px-Kingkamfa.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"King Kamfa","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKingkamfa.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Post-19-1231048922.jpg/500px-Post-19-1231048922.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Post-19-1231048922.jpg/500px-Post-19-1231048922.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kamfamalau","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APost-19-1231048922.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Aequidens_pallidus_MH-MCZArtwork_ARC_20